<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tupamaros</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tupamaros National Liberation Movement</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tupamaros, formally the National Liberation Movement, were a Marxist urban <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> group in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruguay">Uruguay</a> active from the 1960s. Known for robberies, kidnappings and propaganda actions, they were crushed by the armed forces in 1972, shortly before the military seized power in 1973. After democracy returned in 1985 former members entered politics, and one of them, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-mujica">José Mujica</a>, became president in 2010.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tupamaros.jpg" alt="Tupamaros" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Walden69 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bandera_dels_Tupamaros.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The National Liberation Movement, known as MLN-T, was a Marxist-Leninist urban <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> group. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruguay">Uruguay</a> had been one of the most prosperous countries in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a> after the reforms of the Batlle era, but from the mid-1950s falling demand for agricultural exports brought a severe economic crisis. The Tupamaros arose in this period and drew professionals, workers, trade unionists and students.</p><p>One of its leaders, Eleuterio Fernández Huidobro, placed the formal founding in 1965, although its first action was the theft of weapons and ammunition from the Tiro Suizo shooting range in Nueva Helvecia in 1963. The name honoured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tupac-amaru-ii">Túpac Amaru II</a>, who led a large indigenous revolt against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-peru">Viceroyalty of Peru</a> in 1780. Its most important leader was Raúl Sendic.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The movement began with robberies of banks, gun clubs and other businesses, and distributed stolen food and money among the poor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montevideo">Montevideo</a>. Its slogan was &quot;Words divide us; action unites us.&quot; In June 1968 President Jorge Pacheco declared a state of emergency and suspended constitutional safeguards; political dissidents were imprisoned and torture was used in interrogations. In 1969 the Tupamaros carried out the largest robbery in Uruguayan history and occupied the town of Pando on 8 October.</p><p>They then turned to kidnappings, armed propaganda and assassinations. Dan Mitrione, an official who advised the Uruguayan police, was kidnapped on 31 July 1970 and killed on 10 August; the Tupamaros said he had been advising police in torture. The British ambassador Geoffrey Jackson was kidnapped in January 1971. In September 1971 more than 100 imprisoned Tupamaros escaped from Punta Carretas prison through a tunnel, and the government called in the military.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In 1972 the group was crippled. Its violence had weakened popular support, and its wave of assassinations after the killing of four Tupamaros by illegal parapolice squads concentrated opposition against it. After the movement killed soldiers, the army responded with heavy use of torture and persuaded senior members, among them Héctor Amodio Pérez, to collaborate. The group collapsed in mid-1972. According to its own officials about 300 Tupamaros died, mostly in 1972, and about 3,000 were imprisoned. Nine leaders, including Sendic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-mujica">José Mujica</a>, were held in harsh conditions until 1985.</p><p>After the military completed its coup in June 1973, democracy returned in 1985, and amnesty was granted to the Tupamaros and the military. Former members joined the Broad Front, which the movement had helped to form. In 1989 the group was admitted to it, and many members joined the Movement of Popular Participation. Mujica served as president from 2010 to 2015.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tupamaros” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Economic trouble and the <b>Tupamaros</b> led President Jorge Pacheco to declare a state of emergency in 1968 and suspend civil liberties in 1972, and in 1973 the armed forces, asked by President Juan María Bordaberry, disbanded Parliament.</blockquote><a href="/empire/oriental-republic-of-uruguay">Oriental Republic of Uruguay · Into the Present</a></li></ul></section>
